<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trần Nghệ Tông</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1321–<a href="/years/1394">1394</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Tran Nghe Tong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trần Nghệ Tông was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> of the Tran dynasty from 1370 to 1372 and then ruled as retired emperor until his death. He trusted his relative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-quy-ly">Ho Quy Ly</a> with great power, a decision that allowed Ho Quy Ly to depose the Tran and found the Ho dynasty in 1400. His son Tran Ngoi later led resistance to the Ming as Emperor Gian Dinh.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Soon after the Ming conquest of the Ho dynasty in 1407, Tran Ngoi, a son of the Tran emperor <b>Tran Nghe Tong</b>, proclaimed himself emperor Gian Dinh in Ninh Binh and rallied former Tran officials and generals against the occupiers.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-tran-dynasty">Later Tran dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in Imperial Citadel of Thang Long, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.02,105.84&amp;year=1368" aria-label="Hanoi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7396 1386 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Trần Nghệ Tông"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7846 1656h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7846 1656h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.02,105.84&amp;year=1368">What was here in 1368? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
